2.text:
In the stationary mean field the accumulators can be written as the linear
filters of the incoming spike trains.  With the familiar Poisson
white–noise limit

\begin{equation*}
\langle x_{k}(t)x_{l}(t')\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}
+\mu_{k}\mu_{l}c_{kl}\delta(t-t') ,
\end{equation*}
up to the filter overlap, and with $\mu_{k}\equiv\mu,\;w_{k}\equiv w$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu , \qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu\,S_{k},
\end{equation*}
in the continuum limit $S_{k}\to S=\nu\int s(r)\,dr$.
With the instantaneous filter approximation
$\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle =\phi w\sum_{l}s_{kl}
\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle $ we have, with the usual convention
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle=\mu^{2}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]$ and
$c_{kk}\equiv1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
=\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\Bigl[\,S_{k}+1+c\bigl(S_{k}-1\bigr)\Bigr] .
\end{equation*}
The mean weight dynamics is

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle \dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\,\big\langle u_{k}\big\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Bigl[\,S_{k}+1+c\bigl(S_{k}-1\bigr)\Bigr]+\rho S_{k}=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Bigl[1+\frac1S+c\Bigl(1-\frac1S\Bigr)\Bigr]=-\rho .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
c\Bigl(1-\frac1S\Bigr)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1-\frac1S ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. on the usual scale $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{-\,\bigl[1+\rho/\phi\mu\bigr]S-1}{\,S-1\,}
      =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless depression–potentiation ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv -\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
          =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
which is the quantity which is often written in the literature in the
conventionally positive form $\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, $\rho<0$, i.e.
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ up to the overall sign convention for the
offset.  In particular with the sign convention $\dot w_{k}\propto
u_{k}(\rho-v_{k})$ the more familiar positive form
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ is quoted, so that with the usual LTD offset
$\rho<0$

\[
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}<1,
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\]

and with the sign convention used in $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$
this is $\kappa=-(1+\rho/\phi\mu)=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$.  Up to the inessential
overall sign this is the ratio of the effective LTD offset to the
potentiation drive, $\kappa_{0}\approx \rho/\phi\mu+1$, which is $<1$ for
the usual $\rho<0$ and tends to $0$ for strong depression.  This is the
quantity which is normally quoted as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e.
$\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$ in the sign convention of the derivation above.
∎

3.text:
In the mean field the two traces can be replaced by their stationary
means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k}\;, \qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =\sum_{l}s_{kl}w_{l}\mu_{l}\;,
\end{equation*}
and, with the usual Poisson  excess covariance
$\left\langle\! \delta x_{l}\delta x_{k}\!\right\rangle=
c_{kl}\mu_{l}\mu_{k}$, 
\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle
 =\phi w_{k}\mu_{k}\mu_{k}\sum_{l}s_{kl}
   +\phi w_{k}\mu_{k}^{2}c_{kl}\!\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}
  +O(c^{2}) .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\mu_{k}\equiv\mu$, $w_{k}\equiv w$ and $c_{kl}\equiv c$, $k\ne l$,
and writing

\begin{equation*}
{\cal S}_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl}\;, \qquad 
{\cal S}_{k}^{(e)}\equiv\sum_{l\ne k}s_{kl}={\cal S}_{k}-1 ,
\end{equation*}
the mean drift of a synapse is, up to the common factor $w\mu/\tau_{w}$,

\begin{equation*}
 {\cal D}(c)=\phi\mu\Big[{\cal S}_{k}+c\,{\cal S}_{k}^{(e)}\Big]
          +\rho\,{\cal S}_{k}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e.  ${\cal D}(c)=\phi\mu {\cal S}_{k}\big[1+c\big(1-{\cal S}_{k}^{-1}\big)\big]
+\rho {\cal S}_{k}$.  With the  homogeneous  spatial
integral

\begin{equation*}
S\equiv{\cal S}_{k}=1+\nu\!\int\!s\!\left(d\right) {\rm d}d>1 ,
\end{equation*}
${\cal S}_{k}^{(e)}=S-1$, the vanishing of the mean drift
$\langle \dot w_{k}\rangle =0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[S+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional sign of the LTD offset

\begin{equation*}
c\,(S-1)=-S\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the sign–changed correlation which has to be overcome is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= -\,\frac{S}{S-1}\Bigl[1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless LTP/LTD ratio

\begin{equation*}
 \kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
          =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
written in the literature with the LTD offset taken positive,
$\rho\to-\rho_{0}<0$, as

\begin{equation*}
\kappa\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\kappa$ is the ratio of the subtractive LTD offset to the mean
Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  With the usual convention
$\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}\big(v_{k}-\rho_{0}\big)$ this is often quoted
as

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\displaystyle\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}\,S-1}
            {\,S-1\,},\qquad 
\kappa=\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}\to\kappa$ for $S\to\infty$.  Up to the inessential
$+1$ which is absorbed in the definition of $S$ this is the familiar
$ \kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e.  $ \kappa=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu$ for a negative
LTD offset $\rho<0$.  In particular, with the sign convention
$\dot w_{k}\propto u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$ used in most of the dendritic
clustering literature one may write the compact form
$c^{*}=( \kappa S-1)/(S-1)$ with the dimensionless LTD/LTP number
$\kappa=\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$, and $\kappa_{{\rm tot}}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ being
the form quoted above.  The $+1$ is often absorbed in the definition of
$\kappa$ so that the limiting critical correlation for a very wide
spatial spread is simply $c_{\infty}=\kappa$, the usual
$\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$ LTD to LTP ratio. ∎

5.text:
In the fast accumulator limit the two traces can be replaced by their
stationary averages

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \,\langle x_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu ,\qquad 
 \langle u_{k}\rangle =w \mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\equiv w\mu S_{k} ,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual second moment

\begin{equation*}
 \langle x_{l}x_{k}\rangle =\mu^{2}\Big[\,1+c_{kl}\Big]\,,
\qquad c_{kk}=1 ,
\end{equation*}
for the Hebbian drive

\begin{equation*}
\big\langle u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)\big\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\big[\,1+c_{kl}\big]
   +\rho w\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}=0
\end{equation*}
on the $w$ time scale.  Putting $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and
$c_{kl}=c$ for $k\ne l$ and writing

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big) ,
\end{equation*}
the vanishing of the mean weight change is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S_{k}+1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big)\Big]+\rho S_{k}=0 .
\end{equation*}
With the $k$–independent  $S_{k}\equiv S$ this gives for the
spatially averaged correlation

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the usual notation

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}\Big(S-1\Big)=S\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}-1
\end{equation*}
so that in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\,\kappa\,S-1}{S-1},
\qquad 
\kappa\equiv\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless local plasticity gain.  With the sign
convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}\big(v_{k}+\rho\big)$ used in the
text this is often written as $\kappa\to-\kappa$, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm text}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
               =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}=\big(\kappa_{\rm text}S-1\big)/(S-1)$ is the
correlation that makes $\langle\dot w_{k}\rangle=0$.  The $+$ sign
is the usual $ \kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ form; with the sign convention
adopted in the manuscript this is $\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e.
$\kappa\propto\phi\mu+\rho$, the local Hebbian gain reduced by the
depressive offset $\rho$.  Up to the overall sign this is the
commonly quoted $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.  This is the form
$\kappa=-( \phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$ which is quoted in the literature as
$\kappa=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$ in the sign convention of the paper. ∎

