i.e. with $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Bigl[\,1+c_{kl}\Bigr]+\rho S_{k}=0 .
\end{equation*}
Now $s_{kk}=1$ and for $k\ne l$ $c_{kl}=c$.  With the $k$ independent
$S_{k}\equiv S$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1),\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]=S+1+c\,(S-1),
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
c_{{\rm eff}}(S)=\frac{\,1+c\,(S-1)}{S},
\end{equation*}
the fixed point can be written as

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,c_{{\rm eff}}(S)+\rho=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the usual dimensionless homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0,
\qquad c_{{\rm eff}}=\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}=\kappa_{0}\;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\;
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\qquad \kappa_{0}=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
is the LTD/LTP number.  Keeping the self term
$\langle u\rangle\langle v\rangle$ explicitly in the numerator the
gain which is normally quoted is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{{\rm tot}}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that with the conventional positive convention

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{{\rm tot}}S-1}{S-1},
\qquad 
\kappa_{{\rm tot}}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0,
\end{equation*}
which for the usual depressing offset $\rho<0$ is $ \kappa_{{\rm tot}}
<1$ and $c_{\infty}=\kappa_{{\rm tot}}$ for $S\to\infty$.  In the sign
convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}\!\left(v_{k}+\rho\right)$ used in
the statement this is often written with the overall minus sign made
explicit,

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa=-\,\Bigl[\,1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\Bigr]
      =-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $|\kappa|=\bigl(\phi\mu+\rho\bigr)/\phi\mu=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ is the
positive dimensionless Hebbian gain reduced by the subtractive offset
$\rho$.  Absorbing the inessential $+1$ into the definition of $S$ this
is the familiar positive form $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$, i.e. $c^{*}
=( \kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$ with $\kappa_{0}\approx1+\rho/\phi\mu$ for the
usual $|\rho|\ll\phi\mu$.  This is the $\rho/\phi\mu$ LTD to LTP ratio
which is quoted in the literature as $\kappa=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, the minus
sign being a pure convention. ∎

public_text.5:
In the white noise limit one may write on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu_{k},\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w_{l}\mu_{l}\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=1$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
 =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}[1+c_{kl}]\;,
\end{equation*}
up to the filter overlap $\int k_{u}k_{v}$ which can be absorbed in
$\phi$.  With $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big),
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   =\big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle
\end{equation*}
gives on the mean field

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[S_{k}+1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big)\Big]+\rho S_{k}=0 .
\end{equation*}
With the $k$ independent $S_{k}\equiv S$,

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=S\Big[-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1\Big]-1 .
\end{equation*}
Writing the fixed point in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless Hebbian gain which appears in the mean field is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm txt}=-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
       =-\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the $+1$ which is normally absorbed in the definition of $S$
one may write the familiar positive homeostatic ratio

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}
        =\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the overall sign convention $\kappa_{0}=- \rho/\phi\mu>0$
for the usual LTD offset $\rho\to-\rho_{0}<0$.  In particular with the
convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$ used in the
dendritic clustering literature $\rho_{0}=-\rho>0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}=\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}
        =-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\qquad 
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
so that $c_{\infty}=\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}=\kappa_{0}$ is the usual
LTD/LTP ratio $\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$.  The form quoted in the text with the
sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ is
$\kappa_{\rm txt}= -\,[\,1+\rho/\phi\mu\,]$, i.e. $\kappa_{\rm txt}
=-( \phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$, which is the $ \kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$
homeostatic ratio up to the inessential $+1$ and overall sign.  With the
usual LTD sign $\rho<0$ this is the positive number
$\kappa_{0}=1+\rho/\phi\mu=1-|\rho|/\phi\mu$, often quoted simply as the
dimensionless ratio $\kappa=-\rho/\phi\mu=\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$ of the
subtractive LTD offset to the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  The filter
overlap $\int k_{u}k_{v}$ can be absorbed in the definition of $\phi$
and is normally not written explicitly. ∎

public_text.2:
