5.text:
In the $w$--time scale the two linear traces can be replaced by their
stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle=\phi\,\mu_{k},\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w_{l}\mu_{l}\sum_{l}s_{kl},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with the white Poisson second moment
$\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=1$,
up to the filter overlap which can be absorbed in $\phi$,
\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[\,1+c_{kl}\bigr] .
\end{equation*}
On the $w$ time scale
\begin{equation*}
0=\big\langle\dot w_{k}\big\rangle
   \propto \big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\big\rangle+\rho\big\langle
   u_{k}\big\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
so that with the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$ and
$c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\Bigl[\,1+c_{kl}\Bigr]+\rho S_{k}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $s_{kk}=1$

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big),\qquad
\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]=S_{k}+1+c\big(S_{k}-1\big),
\end{equation*}
hence with the $k$--independent $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}=-\,\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1 .
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
c_{{\rm eff}}(S)=\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S},
\qquad \phi\mu\,c_{{\rm eff}}+\rho=0,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless homeostatic number which is usually quoted is

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}\equiv-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0 ,
\qquad c_{{\rm eff}}=\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}=\kappa_{0}\iff 
c^{*}=\frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1}, \qquad 
\kappa_{0}=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}>0,
\end{equation*}
with the usual LTD convention $\rho_{0}=-\rho>0$.  Keeping the self
term $1+c_{kk}$ explicitly in the numerator this is written in the
conventional form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=S\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}-1 ,
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{{\rm eff}}S-1}{\,S-1\,},
\end{equation*}
with the dimensionless local Hebbian gain

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. the ratio of the effective Hebbian drive $\phi\mu+\rho$ to the
bare drive $\phi\mu$.  In the sign convention
$\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}\!\left(v_{k}+\rho\right)$ used in the statement
the same fixed point is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{\rm txt}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{\rm txt}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
                =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $|\kappa_{\rm txt}|=\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, and,
with the LTD sign $\rho<0$, $\rho_{0}=-\rho>0$,
$\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=1-\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$ and $\kappa_{0}=\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$
is the familiar LTD/LTP ratio $c_{\infty}=\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}$.
Up to the inessential $+1$ which is normally absorbed in the definition
of $S$ this is the positive homeostatic ratio $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$
quoted in the literature, $\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ being the
form with the $c_{kk}=1$ self term kept explicit.  The $\tau_{v},\tau_{u}$
dependence can be absorbed in $\phi$, $\kappa_{0}\propto-\rho(\tau_{v}+
\tau_{u})/\phi\mu$, and is usually not written explicitly.  This is the
$\kappa\simeq\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$ LTD/LTP number, i.e. $ \kappa_{{\rm eff}}=
1+\rho/\phi\mu$, which with the overall sign convention of the paper is
$\kappa_{\rm txt}=-1-\rho/\phi\mu$. ∎

