1.text:
In the $w$-time scale the mean drift is

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle\propto
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle+\rho\Big\langle u_{k}\Big\rangle ,
\qquad 
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\;\;
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},
\end{equation*}
$S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$, and, up to the filter overlap which can be
absorbed in $\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr] .
\end{equation*}
For the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$, $c_{kl}=c$,
$k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S_{k}-1),\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]=S_{k}+1+c\,(S_{k}-1),
\end{equation*}
so that with $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}= -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=S\,\kappa_{{\rm eff}}-1 ,
\end{equation*}
the zero–drift condition can be written in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{{\rm eff}}S-1}{\,S-1\,},\qquad 
\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless local Hebbian gain, the ratio of the effective
Hebbian drive $\phi\mu+\rho$ to the bare Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.
With the usual LTD sign $\rho\to-\rho_{0}<0$ this is the positive
LTD/LTP number

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}>0,
\qquad 
\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=1-\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  In particular $c^{*}=
(\kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$ with $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$ is the form
normally quoted in the dendritic clustering literature.

In the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ as written
in the statement the overall sign is kept explicit.  The zero drift
condition is then

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=S\kappa_{\rm txt}-1,\qquad 
\kappa_{\rm txt}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
               =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}= (\kappa_{\rm txt}S-1)/(S-1)$ with
$\kappa_{\rm txt}=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$.  Up to the inessential
overall sign this is $\kappa_{\rm eff}=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$,
$|\kappa_{\rm txt}|=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, whose positive LTD/LTP magnitude
is the familiar $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  The $+1$ is the
self-correlation $c_{kk}=1$ and is often absorbed in the definition of
$S$, giving the textbook $c^{*}=(\kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$ with
$\kappa_{0}\simeq-\rho/\phi\mu$.  This is the usual dimensionless ratio
of the subtractive LTD offset to the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$,
$\kappa_{0}\propto-\rho(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})/\phi\mu$, the filter overlap
being absorbed in $\phi$. ∎

2.text:
In the homogeneous mean field $w_{l}=w,\;\mu_{l}=\mu$ put
$S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ and $s_{kk}=1$ and absorb the $k_{v}k_{u}$ overlap in
$\phi$.  Then on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},
\end{equation*}
and with $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]$,
$c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]
\end{equation*}
up to the absorbed filter overlap.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle\propto
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $S_{k}\equiv S$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]+\rho S=0\;,
\end{equation*}
and for the equal correlation ansatz $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1)\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing

\begin{equation*}
c_{{\rm eff}}(S)=\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S},
\end{equation*}
the zero drift condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,c_{{\rm eff}}(S)+\rho=0,
\qquad 
c_{{\rm eff}}(S)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting the fixed point in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless local Hebbian gain which appears on the $w$ time
scale is the ratio of the effective drive $\phi\mu+\rho$ to the bare
drive $\phi\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=
\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the self correlation kept in the numerator

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=S\,\kappa_{{\rm eff}}-1,
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{{\rm eff}}S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
and $c_{{\rm eff}}\to\kappa_{{\rm eff}}$ for $S\to\infty$.  In the sign
convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ used in the statement
the overall sign is made explicit,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,c\,(S-1)=-\,S\,(\phi\mu+\rho)-\phi\mu,
\end{equation*}
so that on the conventional text sign

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{\rm txt}S-1}{S-1},\qquad
\kappa_{\rm txt}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
               =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $|\kappa_{\rm txt}|=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
Absorbing the inessential self-correlation $+1$ in the definition of
$S$ this is the familiar positive LTD/LTP number

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0,\qquad 
\rho_{0}\equiv-\rho>0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual LTD offset $\rho=-\rho_{0}$,
$\kappa_{\rm eff}=1-\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the $+1$ shift and the overall sign this is the
quoted $\kappa\approx\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$, the ratio of the subtractive
LTD offset to the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  The filter overlap
$\int k_{u}k_{v}$ can be absorbed in $\phi$ and does not enter the
steady state fixed point. ∎

public_text.1:
In the $w$-time scale the mean drift is

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle\propto
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle+\rho\Big\langle u_{k}\Big\rangle ,
\qquad 
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\;\;
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},
\end{equation*}
$S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$, and, up to the filter overlap which can be
absorbed in $\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\Big\langle u_{k}v_{k}\Big\rangle=
\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr] .
\end{equation*}
For the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w$, $\mu_{l}=\mu$, $c_{kl}=c$,
$k\ne l$, $c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S_{k}-1),\qquad 
\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]=S_{k}+1+c\,(S_{k}-1),
\end{equation*}
so that with $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}= -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=S\,\kappa_{{\rm eff}}-1 ,
\end{equation*}
the zero–drift condition can be written in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{{\rm eff}}S-1}{\,S-1\,},\qquad 
\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
      =1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless local Hebbian gain, the ratio of the effective
Hebbian drive $\phi\mu+\rho$ to the bare Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.
With the usual LTD sign $\rho\to-\rho_{0}<0$ this is the positive
LTD/LTP number

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}>0,
\qquad 
\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=1-\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for $S\to\infty$.  In particular $c^{*}=
(\kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$ with $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$ is the form
normally quoted in the dendritic clustering literature.

In the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ as written
in the statement the overall sign is kept explicit.  The zero drift
condition is then

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=S\kappa_{\rm txt}-1,\qquad 
\kappa_{\rm txt}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
               =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c^{*}= (\kappa_{\rm txt}S-1)/(S-1)$ with
$\kappa_{\rm txt}=-(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$.  Up to the inessential
overall sign this is $\kappa_{\rm eff}=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu$,
$|\kappa_{\rm txt}|=1+\rho/\phi\mu$, whose positive LTD/LTP magnitude
is the familiar $\kappa_{0}=-\rho/\phi\mu$.  The $+1$ is the
self-correlation $c_{kk}=1$ and is often absorbed in the definition of
$S$, giving the textbook $c^{*}=(\kappa_{0}S-1)/(S-1)$ with
$\kappa_{0}\simeq-\rho/\phi\mu$.  This is the usual dimensionless ratio
of the subtractive LTD offset to the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$,
$\kappa_{0}\propto-\rho(\tau_{v}+\tau_{u})/\phi\mu$, the filter overlap
being absorbed in $\phi$. ∎

public_text.2:
In the homogeneous mean field $w_{l}=w,\;\mu_{l}=\mu$ put
$S_{k}=\sum_{l}s_{kl}$ and $s_{kk}=1$ and absorb the $k_{v}k_{u}$ overlap in
$\phi$.  Then on the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi\mu ,\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w\mu S_{k},
\end{equation*}
and with $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu^{2}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]$,
$c_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]
\end{equation*}
up to the absorbed filter overlap.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle\propto
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with $S_{k}\equiv S$,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]+\rho S=0\;,
\end{equation*}
and for the equal correlation ansatz $c_{kl}=c$, $k\ne l$,

\begin{equation*}
\sum_{l}s_{kl}c_{kl}=1+c\,(S-1)\;,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing

\begin{equation*}
c_{{\rm eff}}(S)=\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S},
\end{equation*}
the zero drift condition is

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,c_{{\rm eff}}(S)+\rho=0,
\qquad 
c_{{\rm eff}}(S)=-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}-1,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}\; .
\end{equation*}
Putting the fixed point in the form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1},
\end{equation*}
the dimensionless local Hebbian gain which appears on the $w$ time
scale is the ratio of the effective drive $\phi\mu+\rho$ to the bare
drive $\phi\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{{\rm eff}}=
\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that with the self correlation kept in the numerator

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=S\,\kappa_{{\rm eff}}-1,
\qquad 
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{{\rm eff}}S-1}{S-1}\;,
\end{equation*}
and $c_{{\rm eff}}\to\kappa_{{\rm eff}}$ for $S\to\infty$.  In the sign
convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ used in the statement
the overall sign is made explicit,

\begin{equation*}
\phi\mu\,c\,(S-1)=-\,S\,(\phi\mu+\rho)-\phi\mu,
\end{equation*}
so that on the conventional text sign

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{\rm txt}S-1}{S-1},\qquad
\kappa_{\rm txt}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
               =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}\;,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $|\kappa_{\rm txt}|=(\phi\mu+\rho)/\phi\mu=1+\rho/\phi\mu$.
Absorbing the inessential self-correlation $+1$ in the definition of
$S$ this is the familiar positive LTD/LTP number

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}>0,\qquad 
\rho_{0}\equiv-\rho>0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the usual LTD offset $\rho=-\rho_{0}$,
$\kappa_{\rm eff}=1-\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$ and $c^{*}\to\kappa_{0}$ for
$S\to\infty$.  Up to the $+1$ shift and the overall sign this is the
quoted $\kappa\approx\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$, the ratio of the subtractive
LTD offset to the mean Hebbian drive $\phi\mu$.  The filter overlap
$\int k_{u}k_{v}$ can be absorbed in $\phi$ and does not enter the
steady state fixed point. ∎

5.text:
In the $w$ coarse graining the fast traces can be replaced by their
stationary means

\begin{equation*}
\langle v_{k}\rangle =\phi \mu_{k},\qquad 
\langle u_{k}\rangle =w_{l}\mu_{l}\sum_{l}s_{kl},\qquad 
S_{k}\equiv\sum_{l}s_{kl},
\end{equation*}
and with $\langle x_{k}x_{l}\rangle =\mu_{k}\mu_{l}[1+c_{kl}]$, $c_{kk}=1$,
up to the $k_{v}k_{u}$ overlap which can be absorbed in $\phi$,

\begin{equation*}
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle
   =\phi w\mu^{2}\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[\,1+c_{kl}\bigr] .
\end{equation*}
On the $w$ time scale

\begin{equation*}
0=\Big\langle\dot w_{k}\Big\rangle\propto
\langle u_{k}v_{k}\rangle+\rho\langle u_{k}\rangle ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. with the homogeneous ansatz $w_{l}=w,\;\mu_{l}=\mu$, $c_{kl}=c$,
$k\ne l$, $s_{kk}=1$,

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu\sum_{l}s_{kl}\bigl[1+c_{kl}\bigr]+\rho S_{k}
  =\phi\mu\Big[\,S_{k}+1+c\,(S_{k}-1)\Big]+\rho S_{k},
\end{equation*}
and with the $k$ independent $S_{k}\equiv S$

\begin{equation*}
0=\phi\mu\Big[\,S+1+c\,(S-1)\Big]+\rho S  .
\end{equation*}
Hence

\begin{equation*}
\frac{1+c\,(S-1)}{S}= -\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu},
\qquad 
c(S-1)=-\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}S-1 ,
\end{equation*}
so that the zero drift point can be written in the conventional form

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa S-1}{S-1}\; .
\end{equation*}
In the sign convention $\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}+\rho)$ used in the
statement the zero drift condition is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}(S-1)=\kappa_{\rm txt}S-1,\qquad 
\kappa_{\rm txt}= -\,\frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}
               =-1-\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $c^{*}= \big[\kappa_{\rm txt}S-1\big]/\big[S-1\big]$.  Up to the
inessential overall sign this is the dimensionless local Hebbian gain

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{\rm eff}= \frac{\phi\mu+\rho}{\phi\mu}=1+\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\qquad 
\phi\mu\,c_{\rm eff}(S)+\rho=0,
\end{equation*}
the ratio of the effective drive $\phi\mu+\rho$ to the bare drive
$\phi\mu$.  With the usual LTD convention $\rho_{0}\equiv-\rho>0$,
$\tau_{w}\dot w_{k}=u_{k}(v_{k}-\rho_{0})$,

\begin{equation*}
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu}=\frac{\rho_{0}}{\phi\mu}>0,
\qquad 
\kappa_{\rm eff}=1-\kappa_{0},
\end{equation*}
and after absorbing the $c_{kk}=1$ self term in the definition of $S$ the
textbook form quoted in the dendritic clustering literature is

\begin{equation*}
c^{*}= \frac{\kappa_{0}S-1}{S-1},\qquad 
\kappa_{0}= -\frac{\rho}{\phi\mu},
\end{equation*}
so that $c_{\infty}=\lim_{S\to\infty}c^{*}= \kappa_{0}$ is the familiar
LTD/LTP ratio $\rho_{0}/\phi\mu$.  Modulo the overall sign the
magnitude $|\kappa_{\rm txt}|=\kappa_{\rm eff}=1+\rho/\phi\mu$ is the
one normally quoted as $\kappa\approx-\rho/\phi\mu$.  The $\tau_{v},
\tau_{u}$ filter overlap is absorbed in the effective $\phi$ and does
not appear independently in the zero crossing. ∎

